course: physics
lesson: 24
title: Melting, freezing and boiling
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Water can be solid ice, liquid water, or an invisible gas called water vapour, and adding or taking away heat moves it from one to another.
explain: Melting ice soaks up a lot of heat without getting any warmer, so the ice cubes in your squash keep it near freezing for a long time.
explain: In a kettle at 100 degrees, water turns into steam right inside the liquid and rises as big bubbles; the white cloud from the spout is tiny droplets of cooled steam, and both can scald.
explain: When water vapour in the air touches something cold, like a chilled can or a winter window, it turns back into droplets; the water came from the air, not through the can.
explain: Most things grow a little when hot and shrink when cold, so bridges have gaps with metal teeth, and a stuck jar lid loosens under the hot tap.
fact: At 100 degrees, water boils: it turns into steam all through the liquid, which rises as big bubbles.
fact: Steam is invisible; the white cloud you see is tiny droplets formed as steam cools, and both steam and that cloud can scald.
fact: A cold can gets wet outside because water vapour in the air condenses into droplets on the cold metal.
fact: Windows steam up on a cold morning because warm, damp air inside condenses on the cold glass, the same way your breath makes a cloud in winter.
fact: Melting ice takes in lots of heat without getting warmer, so a drink with ice stays near 0 degrees until the ice is gone.
fact: Fresh water freezes at 0 degrees, and water grows as it turns to ice, which is why frozen pipes can burst.
fact: Chocolate melts just below body temperature, so it melts in your pocket and in your mouth but stays hard on a cool shelf.
fact: A fridge or freezer does not make cold; it pumps heat out of the box and lets it go at the back, which feels warm, and its motor adds a little heat of its own.
fact: Bridges grow a little in summer and shrink in winter, and the gaps with metal teeth stop them cracking or buckling.
fact: A stuck metal jar lid opens after running it under hot water, because metal grows more than glass when warmed.
fact: Snow does not melt the moment the Sun comes out, because white snow bounces most sunlight away and melting needs a lot of heat.
fact: Dirty snow melts faster than clean snow, because the dark dirt soaks up sunlight.
exercise: At what temperature does water boil at sea level? | 100 degrees | About 80 degrees | Exactly 50 degrees | Only 37 degrees
exercise: What are the big bubbles in a boiling kettle made of? | Steam forming inside the water | Air that was trapped in the kettle | Bits of the metal heating element | Soap left from washing the kettle
exercise: What is the white cloud coming out of a kettle's spout? | Tiny droplets formed as steam cools | Smoke coming off the hot metal inside | Pure invisible steam, still very hot | Dust blown out of the kettle's spout
exercise: Why does a cold can of drink get wet on the outside? | Water vapour in the air turns to droplets on the cold can | The cold drink leaks out slowly through tiny holes in the metal | The can sweats like a person | The ice inside melts through the can
exercise: Why do windows steam up on a cold morning? | Warm damp air inside condenses on the cold glass | Rain from outside soaks slowly through the window glass | The glass melts a little in the cold | Frost inside the glass melts
exercise: Why do ice cubes keep a drink cold for a long time? | Melting ice takes in lots of heat without warming | Ice gives out cold rays into the drink all the time | Ice makes the drink heavier and slower | Ice stops the air touching the drink
exercise: At what temperature does fresh water freeze? | 0 degrees | 10 degrees | Minus 50 degrees | 37 degrees
exercise: Why can a water pipe burst on a freezing night? | Water grows as it turns to ice | Ice is much heavier than water | Cold metal always snaps | The pipe shrinks onto the water
exercise: Why does chocolate melt in your pocket? | It melts just below body temperature | Pockets are heated by your phone battery | Chocolate melts at any temperature at all | The cloth pulls the chocolate apart slowly
exercise: How does a freezer freeze a lolly? | It pumps heat out of the box and lets it go at the back | It blows cold air in from outside the house through a pipe | It makes cold out of electricity | It squeezes the lolly until it is hard
exercise: Why is the back of a fridge warm? | That is where the heat taken from inside is let out | The fridge is broken | The light inside heats it | The food inside is warming it
exercise: Why do bridges have gaps with metal teeth? | The bridge grows in summer and shrinks in winter | To let rainwater drain through | To stop cars going too fast | To make a loud noise as cars cross, to warn drivers
exercise: Why does a stuck jar lid open after running it under hot water? | The metal lid grows more than the glass | The hot water melts the glue that holds the lid | The glass gets soft in hot water | The jam inside pushes the lid off
exercise: Why does snow not melt the moment the Sun comes out? | White snow bounces most sunlight away and melting needs much heat | Snow is colder than ice | The Sun cannot shine on snow | Snow is made of a different water
exercise: Which snow melts faster in the sunshine? | Dirty snow, because the dark dirt soaks up sunlight | Clean white snow | Both exactly the same | Neither, sunlight cannot melt snow at all, only warm air
exercise: Can steam you cannot see still burn you? | Yes, invisible steam can scald | No, only the white cloud is hot | No, steam is always cool | Only if it is coloured
test: me mate says the can's leaking cos it's wet outside. right? | No, the water came from the air and condensed on the cold can | Yes, cans always leak when cold | Yes, the drink seeps through the metal | No, it is sweat from his hand
test: Ugh, left the door of the fridge open to cool the kitchen on a hot day. Will that work? | No, the fridge just moves heat round the kitchen and adds its own | Yes, it will cool the whole house | Yes, if you leave it open all night | No, fridges only work at night
test: What would happen if a bridge were built with no gaps at all? | It could crack or buckle as it grows in summer | It would be stronger in every way, with no weak points | It would shrink away to nothing | Nothing at all would happen
test: A drink with ice and a drink of cold water start at the same temperature. Which stays cold longer? | The one with ice, because melting soaks up heat | The cold water one | Both exactly the same | Neither, both warm up instantly to room temperature
test: In a cartoon a snowman melts in seconds when the Sun comes out. What is closer to the truth? | Snow melts slowly, because it reflects sunlight and melting needs lots of heat | Snow melts the instant sunlight touches it | Snow never melts in sunshine | Snow melts only at night
test: Why does your breath make a little cloud on a cold day but not on a warm one? | The cold air turns the vapour in your breath into droplets | Your breath is hotter in winter | Your breath is smokier in winter | Your lungs only make little clouds in winter, to keep you warm
test: Why do people put salt or grit on icy roads rather than wait for the Sun? | The Sun alone often melts ice slowly | Salt makes the Sun shine harder on the road | The Sun only makes ice thicker and harder | Salt stops the road from shrinking in the cold
test: You watch the thermometer in a glass of melting ice for ten minutes. What do you see? | It stays near 0 degrees while the ice melts | It rises steadily the whole time | It falls lower and lower | It jumps straight up to room temperature at once
test: Why is it a bad idea to put a full glass bottle of water in the freezer? | Water grows as it freezes and can crack the bottle | Glass melts in the freezer | The water will boil in the cold and blow the cap off | The freezer will stop working
test: oi, don't stick your hand over the spout, the white stuff's just mist innit. What's the truth? | It is scalding hot, and the invisible steam below it is too | It is cool mist and harmless | Only the kettle body is hot | It is dust and will just make you sneeze
test: Your dad holds a stuck jar lid under the hot tap and it opens. Which idea explains it best? | Metal grows more than glass when warmed | Hot water dissolves the lid | Glass grows more than metal when warmed | Hot water makes the jam lighter
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the external surface of a chilled beverage can becomes wet. | Water vapour in the ambient air condenses upon the cold surface | The beverage permeates the metal of the can | The metal itself liberates water | The can is wetted by the warm hand that holds it, which perspires
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the operation of a domestic refrigerator? | It transfers heat from its interior and releases it at its rear | It generates cold from electrical energy | It admits cold air from outside the dwelling | It compresses foodstuffs in order to cool them more rapidly and evenly
exam: It is given that water is enclosed in a sealed rigid vessel and frozen. State the likely consequence. | The vessel may fracture owing to the expansion of the water upon freezing | The vessel will contract and remain sound | The water will contract and leave a void | No alteration of volume will occur
exam: Ascertain the reason for which expansion joints are incorporated into bridges. | To accommodate the expansion and contraction of the structure with temperature | To permit the drainage of rainwater exclusively | To reduce the speed of vehicles | To generate a warning sound
